    },
    {
      "id": "f1f49b8b-9874-46dc-a097-75787222c520",
      "phaseId": "09bcb3cf-f8ea-454a-913e-ea377c53d37e",
      "number": 316,
      "shortId": "5HZCT",
      "priority": 6,
      "shortName": "generate-plugin-facing-type-de",
      "title": "Generate plugin-facing type definitions (.d.ts / JSON Schema) from the widget vocabulary",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "User's proposal, 2026-07-25: a script that emits a `.d.ts` so plugin authors working in TypeScript\nget real types instead of hand-writing untyped JSON.\n\n═══ WHY IT FITS ═══\nA `ViewNode` is data with a tag on it, which is what TypeScript's discriminated unions are for. One\ngenerated file gives per-widget checking with no builder objects at all:\n\n    export type PropValue = boolean | number | string;\n    export type Intent = { action: string; args?: Record<string, PropValue> };\n\n    export type ViewNode =\n      | { kind: \"column\"; props?: { gap?: number; padding?: number; align?: Align };\n          children?: ViewNode[] }\n      | { kind: \"input\";  props?: { text?: string; placeholder?: string; name?: string };\n          events?: { change?: Intent } }\n      | { kind: \"scroll\"; props?: { axes?: \"vertical\" | \"horizontal\" | \"both\" };\n          children?: ViewNode[] }\n      | { kind: \"button\"; props?: { text?: string; variant?: Variant };\n          events?: { press?: Intent }; children?: ViewNode[] }\n      // …all 30 kinds\n\nAn author then writes plain objects and is still caught:\n    { kind: \"label\", props: { placeholder: \"…\" } }\n    // Object literal may only specify known properties — 'placeholder' does not exist on\n    // type '{ text?: string; bold?: boolean; ... }'\nSame guarantee as the Rust builders, and events are typed too — `press` exists only on widgets that\ncan be pressed.\n\n═══ HARD CONDITION — GENERATED FROM THE ONE SOURCE, AND GUARDED ═══\nA hand-written `.d.ts` would be a FOURTH place to forget a property, which is the exact defect\nF003/P017 exists to remove, restated in another language. It must be generated from the same\nproperty table that F003/P017/T3 produces, and it needs the same style of guard: regenerate during\nthe build and FAIL if the checked-in file differs from what the source produces. Without that it is\nstale the first time someone adds a widget.\n⇒ PREREQUISITE: F003/P017/T3 (generated translation + drift test). Before that table exists there is\nnothing to generate from. Check there that T3's tables are reusable outside the app rather than\nprivate to it — that check is already noted in F003/P017/T9.\nThe existing `WidgetKind::ALL` coverage guard (heca/src/chrome/view.rs:78) already guarantees the\nkind list is complete, so the generator can trust it.\n\n═══ THE REAL BLOCKER: WHAT IS A PLUGIN, TECHNICALLY? ═══\nTypeScript does NOT compile to WebAssembly. A TS plugin needs either a JavaScript engine running\ninside WASM, or a different plugin model where the plugin is a separate process. Nobody has decided\nwhich. Until that is settled we would be generating types for a plugin that cannot run.\nThis is the SAME underlying question as T1's (where the plugin-facing types live) and\nF003/P017/T9's. Answer \"what is a plugin\" once, and the artifact choice falls out of it.\nDO NOT start this task before that decision.\n\n═══ WHICH ARTIFACT — DECIDE WITH THE USER, DO NOT ASSUME ═══\n- `.d.ts` — for TypeScript plugin authors. Blocked on the question above.\n- JSON Schema — the cheaper cousin, generated from the same table. Serves TOML/JSON tooling and any\n  schema-aware editor TODAY, and is the better fit for F003/P009 (plugin-07: simple config.toml\n  plugins), which are TOML and would otherwise get no help at all.\n- Both, from one generator with two emitters.\nRecommendation to put to the user: JSON Schema first (it has a consumer that exists), `.d.ts` when\nthe plugin model is settled.\n\n═══ STATE OF THE CONSUMERS (verified 2026-07-25) ═══\n- No schema/type export exists anywhere: no `schemars`, no `JsonSchema`, no `.d.ts`, no typescript\n  reference in heca, heca-grid-ui or heca-config.\n- `heca/src/rpc.rs` is rich — it takes action names and returns JSON, and `describe-action` already\n  returns structured `ActionInfo`. But NOTHING in it accepts a description tree, so there is no\n  external caller for these types yet. If an RPC command that accepts a `ViewNode` is ever added,\n  that becomes an immediate consumer and this task gets much more valuable.\n\n═══ ACCEPTANCE (once unblocked) ═══\n- Type definitions are generated from the same table as the Rust side; no hand-maintained list.\n- A build-time check fails when the checked-in output does not match a fresh generation.\n- Every one of the 30 kinds appears, with its properties AND its events, per-kind — a property on\n  the wrong widget must be a type error, not silently accepted.\n- Enum-valued properties emit as string unions (e.g. \"vertical\" | \"horizontal\" | \"both\"), matching\n  how they travel in the model.\n- Documented for authors wherever the plugin authoring guide ends up after F003/P017/T8.",
      "notes": "",
